---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import ServiceCard from '../../components/ServiceCard.astro';
import CTABlock from '../../components/CTABlock.astro';

const related = [
  { title: 'Site Preparation', description: 'Clearing, grading, and prepping your lot before the build starts.', href: '/services/site-preparation' },
  { title: 'Foundation Excavation', description: 'Digging and leveling for footings and foundations.', href: '/services/foundation-excavation' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="How to Prepare Land for Construction | D&D Land Works"
	description="The order site work happens in before a foundation goes in: clearing, grading, drainage, and compaction. What to expect building in Lane County, OR."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="How-To"
		headline="How to Prepare Land for Construction"
		subheadline="Site prep isn't one task, it's a sequence. Skip a step or do it out of order and the next trade inherits the problem."
		primaryCta={{ label: 'Call 541-401-8726', href: 'tel:5414018726' }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: 'Free Estimate', href: '/contact' }}
	/>
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16">
		<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			The Order Matters
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			A lot that looks build-ready from the road usually isn't. Trees and brush have to come out
			before anyone can see the actual grade. The grade has to get set before drainage gets figured
			out, because water follows the slope, not the plans. And none of that holds up under a
			foundation until it's compacted properly. Build out of order and you end up regrading a pad
			that was already poured around, or trenching a drain line through ground that just got seeded.
		</p>
		<h2 class="mt-12 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			The Steps, in Order
		</h2>
		<ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-slate-700 list-decimal list-inside">
			<li><strong>Clear the site.</strong> Trees, stumps, brush, and debris come out first. Nothing else can be measured accurately until the ground underneath is visible.</li>
			<li><strong>Rough grade.</strong> Cut and fill get the site to the general elevation the plans call for, with material moved on-site or hauled if there's too much or too little.</li>
			<li><strong>Plan for drainage.</strong> Swales, culverts, or a French drain get sited based on where water naturally wants to go once the rough grade is in.</li>
			<li><strong>Compact.</strong> Loose fill settles unevenly under a foundation. Compaction equipment packs it down before anything gets built on top of it.</li>
			<li><strong>Finish grade.</strong> A final pass sets the exact pad elevation and slope a foundation contractor or landscaper needs to start their part.</li>
		</ol>
		<h2 class="mt-12 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink">
			What This Looks Like in Lane County
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
			A wooded South Hills lot in Eugene needs more clearing before anything else can start. A flat
			ag parcel out toward Junction City might skip straight to grading. Every county building permit
			assumes the pad underneath it is actually solid, not just leveled by eye. D&D Land Works handles
			the sequence start to finish, licensed and bonded under CCB #261742, serving Eugene, Springfield,
			and the rest of <a href="/service-area" class="text-brand-blue underline hover:text-brand-blue-dark">Lane County</a>.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pb-16">
		<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-2xl text-ink text-center">
			Related Services
		</h2>
		<div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
			{related.map((service) => <ServiceCard {...service} />)}
		</div>
	</section>
	<CTABlock
		headline="Get Your Site Build-Ready"
		subheadline="Free estimate for site preparation anywhere in Lane County."
	/>
</BaseLayout>
